WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.300
You might have seen this
recruitment poster before.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.855
But what if it said
something like this?

3
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.040
Hmm.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.360
Not quite as effective,
is it?

5
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.230
Not so compelling.

6
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:18.750
It would be much easier
to just walk past that one.

7
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.840
Apart from old Uncle Sam's
pointing finger,

8
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.190
what's the key thing missing
from this poor imitation poster?

9
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.060
Yep.

10
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.790
It's 'you'.

11
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.240
It's hard to ignore
the word 'you'.

12
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.720
And yet, when we write at work,
all too often we do.

13
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.430
And, you know, you shouldn't!

14
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.710
Like Uncle Sam in the poster,
the word 'you'

15
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.500
points directly out of your document
at the reader.

16
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.120
It says, 'This means you.'

17
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.910
This is relevant to you.

18
00:00:53.910 --> 00:01:00.230
No, not that guy.
Not people in general. You.

19
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.370
A lot of the time when we write,
we want something

20
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.480
to happen as a result.
We want people to act.

21
00:01:-09.480 --> 00:01:11.580
For that to happen,
those people need

22
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.130
to know you're talking to them.

23
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.490
You're not making a passing remark.

24
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.580
Think about the difference between something

25
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.210
like, 'Say, it'd be nice
if someone could pass me a pen.'

26
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.780
And something like,
'Could you please pass me a pen?'

27
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.390
Hey, thanks.

28
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.206
OK, the reaction could also be,
'Get it yourself!'

29
00:01:39.206 --> 00:01:41.070
But that's still a reaction.

30
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.730
Being indirect is a great way
to get no response.

31
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.980
It's too easy to ignore.

32
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.720
Speak to everyone in general
and you essentially

33
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.690
speak to no one in particular.

34
00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.260
OK. Um ...

35
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.580
Does that mean me?

36
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.330
Ah!

37
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:02.430
OK.

38
00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:06.400
So when you write at work,
remember Uncle Sam's lesson:

39
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.009
talk to your reader directly.

40
00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.490
And yes, this means you.